WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.858
Your program runs, and the dog does
the wrong thing. That is a bug.

2
00:00:09.078 --> 00:00:13.629
Run it, find it, fix it, and run
it again. Ask a friend to look.

3
00:00:14.129 --> 00:00:18.030
The goal is written at the
top: grow, then jump twice.

4
00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:21.919
Here is the program. Run it first
and watch what the dog does.

5
00:00:22.139 --> 00:00:26.086
The dog grows. Then it
spins. Then it jumps once.

6
00:00:26.306 --> 00:00:29.882
That is not the goal. Watching
it told us where to look.

7
00:00:30.102 --> 00:00:34.746
The second block spins. We wanted
a jump. That block is the bug.

8
00:00:34.966 --> 00:00:38.589
Take the spin block out. Put
a jump block in its place.

9
00:00:38.809 --> 00:00:42.570
Now run it again. Grow, jump,
jump. That is what we wanted.

10
00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:48.550
Some programs have two bugs. The
goal: move left twice, then say hello.

11
00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:52.114
Run it. The dog goes left,
then right, then it spins.

12
00:00:52.334 --> 00:00:55.724
The second block goes right.
We wanted a second move left.

13
00:00:55.944 --> 00:01:00.216
Fix that block. It is
better now. But is it right?

14
00:01:00.436 --> 00:01:04.244
Only running it again can tell
you. Left, left, and then a spin.

15
00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:08.876
Still not hello. The last
block spins: a second bug.

16
00:01:09.096 --> 00:01:14.158
Fix it, run it again, and the dog
says hello. Test after every fix.

17
00:01:14.658 --> 00:01:18.373
Sami has read his program ten
times. He cannot see the bug.

18
00:01:18.593 --> 00:01:23.191
Amal looks at it once. Fresh
eyes see what tired eyes miss.

19
00:01:23.411 --> 00:01:26.801
One of you can read the blocks.
The other watches the dog.

20
00:01:27.021 --> 00:01:31.201
If you are stuck, press Ask.
Your partner gives you a hint.

21
00:01:31.421 --> 00:01:35.182
Asking is not failing. Programmers
ask each other all day.

22
00:01:35.682 --> 00:01:39.212
Explaining your program out
loud is a way of debugging.

23
00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:44.865
Sami reads his blocks, one at
a time. Move right. Jump. Spin.

24
00:01:45.085 --> 00:01:48.986
Spin? We wanted jump. He
hears the wrong block himself.

25
00:01:49.206 --> 00:01:52.689
Your ears can catch a bug your
eyes walked straight past.

26
00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:56.206
Say each block slowly.
Does it match the goal?

27
00:01:56.706 --> 00:01:59.818
Real programmers work
in pairs and in teams.

28
00:02:00.038 --> 00:02:04.357
Two of them share one screen. One
types, and one watches for bugs.

29
00:02:04.577 --> 00:02:08.617
They read each other's programs,
to find bugs the writer missed.

30
00:02:08.837 --> 00:02:12.738
In a games team, one person's
whole job is to find bugs.

31
00:02:12.958 --> 00:02:17.509
Debugging together is how big
programs get made. So can you.

32
00:02:18.009 --> 00:02:21.074
Run it first, and find
the block that is wrong.

33
00:02:21.294 --> 00:02:25.288
A program can have two bugs,
so test after every fix.

34
00:02:25.508 --> 00:02:29.177
Ask a partner to look, and
explain your blocks out loud.

35
00:02:29.397 --> 00:02:31.904
Two heads find a bug faster than one.
